# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — it no longer exists in .NET 5+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` is also gone** — the entire cooperative abort model was removed
3. **`ThreadAbortException` will never be thrown** — catch blocks for it are dead code

## Why It Was Removed

`Thread.Abort()` was inherently unsafe — it could interrupt code at any point (including inside `finally` blocks, locks, and unmanaged calls), corrupting state in ways that were nearly impossible to debug.

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask Stop(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait for graceful completion with a timeout
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Log warning: worker did not exit within timeout
            // Unlike Thread.Abort, we can't forcibly kill it — design
            // GetNextItemAsync to respect the token so this won't happen
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Migration Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Details |
|---------|---------|
| **Blocking calls ignore cancellation** | If `GetNextItem()` blocks on a `Monitor.Wait` or synchronous I/O, cancellation won't interrupt it. You **must** convert to async APIs that accept `CancellationToken`. |
| **Fire-and-forget `Task.Run`** | Unobserved exceptions silently disappear. Always `await` the worker task during shutdown. |
| **`volatile bool` is insufficient** | A `CancellationToken` is superior — it integrates with async APIs, `WaitHandle`, and `linked tokens` for hierarchical cancellation. |
| **No forced termination exists** | If code truly hangs (e.g., unmanaged call), your only recourse is process-level termination or running the work in a separate process. Design for cooperation. |
| **`Thread.Sleep` in Stop()** | Replace with `await Task.WhenAny(...)` — non-blocking and more precise. |

## If You Can't Go Fully Async

For sync-only code paths that must remain cancellable:

```csharp
private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
{
    while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        // Poll-based cancellation for sync blocking calls
        while (!HasNextItem())
        {
            token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
            Thread.Sleep(50); // cooperative polling
        }

        var item = GetNextItem();
        ProcessItem(item);
    }
}
```

## Summary

The core principle changes from **preemptive** (caller forces stop) to **cooperative** (worker checks and respects cancellation). This is safer and more predictable, but requires all blocking operations to participate in the cancellation protocol.
